WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.920
Alright, so you've made commits, you
understand that a commit ID is a big

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:09.960
deal and now we're going to talk about a
two-step commit: why you have to use

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:15.330
'git add' and 'git commit'. Now remember when we make a commit ID that is pretty

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:17.430
permanent, right, that's in history.

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:22.109
So, this section over here is our log of
commits. It's like a story of all the

00:00:22.109 --> 00:00:26.910
changes you've made to put together your
code base. But first, we have to get there,

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:31.710
right? So, you're working locally you make
some changes. Say you make changes to an

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:37.290
index.html and to a CSS file but you
don't really want those to be stored

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:42.390
together - the changes that you made were not logically related in anyway - that's

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:48.000
ok. That's what 'get add' is for. Git lets
you choose exactly which files you want

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:53.100
to include in the next commit so let's
say we want to do the index first so

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:58.649
we'll 'git add index.html' and they'll move
that file and git's metadata to the

00:00:58.649 --> 00:01:03.899
staging area. Git knows that when you run
'git commit' only to include the index

00:01:03.899 --> 00:01:09.960
file not the CSS file. So, any files in
the working directory, if you want them

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:14.220
to be included in the commit you need to
use 'git add' first. Once they're in the

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:18.180
staging area, Git's going to include them
in the next commit once you run

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:23.430
'git commit' and then all of those files, that snapshot of the repository, including

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:27.540
your changes, are stored with that 40
character sha-1 hash in history.

